\title[CM and the residue shadow of elliptic point counts]{Complex Multiplication and the Residue Shadow of Elliptic Point Counts}

\subjclass[2020]{11G20, 11T24, 11Y05, 14H52, 94A17}
\keywords{complex multiplication, supersingular reduction, trace of Frobenius,
quadratic character sums, elliptic curve factorization, $p+1$ method,
mutual information}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{14 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
Let $N=pq$ be a semiprime and $\ell$ a small prime. For an elliptic curve $E$
one may ask how much information the residue $N \bmod m$ carries about the
divisibility events $\ell \mid \#E(\FF_p)$ and $\ell \mid \#E(\FF_q)$. For a
curve with full mod-$\ell$ image the answer is: none measurable. We show that
this invisibility is a \emph{generic} phenomenon by exhibiting, for curves with
complex multiplication, an exact structural mechanism that partially restores
it, and by proving that what is restored is precisely the abelian channel
already exploited by the $p+1$ method.

We give complete elementary proofs of the following. For $\Emfour: y^2=x^3+x$
and any odd prime $p$, the trace of Frobenius satisfies $a_p=0$ if and only if
$p\equiv 3 \pmod 4$, and $4 \mid \#\Emfour(\FF_p)$ for every odd $p$; the
``only if'' direction is derived as a corollary of the mod-$4$ point count
rather than from complex-multiplication theory. The same argument shows that
the whole quartic-twist family $y^2=x^3+Ax$ has exactly $p+1$ points at every
inert prime, and yields the quadratic-twist relation
$\#E+\#E^{u}=2p+2$. For $\Emthree: y^2=x^3+1$ and any prime
$p\notin\{2,3\}$, $a_p=0$ if and only if $p \equiv 2 \pmod 3$; on the inert
half the entire family $y^2=x^3+B$ has $p+1$ points, and on the split half
$3 \mid \#\Emthree(\FF_p)$ via a free $\ZZ/3$-action off the fibre over $x=0$.

We then quantify the residue shadow. The symmetric channel is genuinely live:
if $p,q \equiv 3 \pmod 4$ and $pq \equiv 5 \pmod{12}$ then
$3 \mid \#\Emfour(\FF_p)$ or $3\mid\#\Emfour(\FF_q)$; empirically this is
$0.0048$ and $0.0062$ bits of mutual information at $\ell=3,5$, about $4.8$
times the noise floor, against $0.0000$ for a non-CM curve on the same data.
The asymmetric (which-factor) channel is nonetheless empty: $77 \equiv 209
\pmod{12}$ with identical symmetric values and opposite least-factor bits.
Even the symmetric channel is only partial; for $\Emthree$ at $\ell=5$ it is
dead; and on the inert half the elliptic stage-one divisibility test
\emph{is} the $p+1$ test. The verdict is a confirmed null for factoring:
complex multiplication restores a residue shadow, but only the abelian one.
\end{abstract}

